Show every Chromium frame, one per Qt frame (Omarchy Mobile).

Qt takes a frame only when its GUI thread, which is also Chromium's browser
thread, has passed on the frame's update request; when that came late, the
next frame replaced the one waiting, and the replaced frame was never shown:
on the X web app about a third of Chromium's frames during a fling
(QTWEBENGINE_FRAME_STATS: 189 of 600 presents found Qt still holding the
previous one), so the screen went between 120 and 60 frames a second.

Finished frames now wait in a queue of two, as in Android's buffer queue:
Qt takes the oldest at each of its frames, and the item asks Qt for another
frame while one is still waiting. A late Qt frame shows the frame it missed
one refresh later instead of dropping it. Chromium waits only when two
frames wait already (a window that is not drawn still stops it), so it is
throttled to Qt's rate rather than a frame behind every time. The cost is up
to a frame of latency while Qt is behind.

